    "created_at": "2016-08-17T15:44:05Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-08-17T15:52:56Z",
    "body": "Yeah, it might be useful to define a CSS parsing mode that doesn't allow CSS escapes, so we can rely on standard CSS parsing behavior (rules for idents, etc) without needing to allow the escape weirdness in HTML/JS stuff.  It's a relatively easy edit to Syntax.\n\nTho the important question is why it's important to disallow escapes? I presume it's because HTML and JS already have methods of escaping, and you don't want to layer two of them?  The two aren't _strictly_ equivalent - CSS escapes allow you to put ASCII characters in places that aren't normally valid (like, `class=\"12\"` in HTML needs `.\\31 2` in CSS) - but if all we care about is Unicode characters, then yeah, JS/HTML escapes give the same power as CSS escapes.\n",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
